# Untitled

## Adsorptive denitrogenation of model fossil fuels with Lewis acid-loaded metal–organic frameworks (MOFs)
Imteaz Ahmed, Jong Won Jun, Beom K. Jung, Sung Hwa Jhung
Department of Chemistry and Green-Nano Materials Research Center, Kyungpook National University, Daegu 702-701, Republic of Korea
## H I G H L I G H T S
• Lewis acid-loaded MOFs were prepared firstly by impregnation. • Acidic MOFs were effective in adsorption of basic N-compounds. • The favorable adsorption was due to an acid–base interaction. • Acidic MOFs were not effective in adsorption of neutral N-compounds.
G R A P H I C A L A B S T R A C T
## a r t i c l e i n f o
Article history:  Received 17 May 2014  Received in revised form 19 June 2014  Accepted 23 June 2014  Available online 30 June 2014
Keywords:  Adsorptive denitrogenation  Lewis acid  Acid–base interaction  Metal–organic frameworks
## a b s t r a c t
A metal–organic framework (MOF), MIL-100(Fe), was impregnated with a Lewis acidic salt, \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\), in order to prepare an acidic adsorbent. The adsorbent was used in adsorptive denitrogenation (ADN) of model fuels to investigate the effect of the Lewis acidity of the composite adsorbent. To provide more in-depth understanding of the interaction/adsorption mechanism, a basic quinoline (QUI) and a neutral indole (IND) were utilized as adsorbates. A sulfur-containing compound, benzothiophene (BT), was also used along with the two nitrogen-containing compounds (NCCs) in order to investigate the selectivity of the adsorption of the NCCs. The maximum adsorption capacity of \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}/\mathsf{M I L}{-}100(\mathsf{F e})\) for QUI was \(17\%\) higher than that of the pristine MIL-100(Fe), despite an \(8\%\) reduction of the surface area upon loading the MOF with \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\). In contrast, the performance of the \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}/\mathsf{M I L}{-}100(\mathsf{F e})\) adsorbents in the adsorption of neutral IND decreased with the content of \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\) due to little interaction between the acidic adsorbent and neutral IND and the low porosities of the modified adsorbents. Therefore, the investigation shows that MOFs impregnated with Lewis acidic materials such as \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\) can be effectively used for adsorptive removal of basic NCCs by means of an acid–base interaction. It can also be concluded that MOFs, when modified suitably with specific functional materials, can be used for specific and improved adsorption.
