## 1. Introduction
Due to the continuous growth of the world's population and consumption of energy, combustion of fossil fuels has also continued to increase, and therefore, the optimized utilization of energy is one of the greatest challenges of this century. Under these cir
cumstances, new and unusual sources of fossil fuels must be exploited in the future to address the high energy requirements. Thus, fuels containing large amounts of contaminants, especially sulfur- and nitrogen-containing compounds (NCCs and SCCs, respectively), are expected to be utilized [1–3]. However, NCCs and SCCs in fossil fuels are among the greatest environmental threats in the present world. Upon combustion of NCCs and SCCs, \(\mathsf{S O}_{x}\) and \(\mathsf{N O}_{x}\) are produced, and these compounds are directly expelled into the atmosphere in most cases, which causes several
environmental problems, including creation of acid rain, adverse effects to biodiversity, and human health complications.
Over the last few decades, the removal of SCCs from fossil fuels has been extensively studied, and the methods of removal are quite well established. SCCs are conventionally removed by hydrodesulfurization (HDS) process. However, before the removal of SCCs by HDS, NCCs should be removed, as they have an adverse effect on both the catalysts and the conventional HDS process [4–6]. NCCs are generally removed by hydrodenitrogenation (HDN), which is a costly and energy intensive process due to the requirements for expensive hydrogen and high temperature/pressure. Additionally, HDN is an inefficient process compared with HDS because the nitrogen in NCCs can be removed only after the heterocyclic rings have been hydrogenated, and hence, HDN is a kinetically slow multistep process [4,5,7]. Consequently, development of an economically viable, selective removal procedure for NCCs is very important, and adsorptive denitrogenation (ADN) may be a possible alternative for the removal of NCCs. ADN, unlike HDN, is a low cost process that does not require expensive hydrogen and can be performed at ambient temperature and pressure. Several materials have been studied for use in the ADN process. These include activated carbon [4,5,8–11], Cu(I)-Y zeolites [6,12], ion exchange resins [13], meso-silicas [14], Ti-HMSs [15], microporous carbons [16], activated aluminas [10] and Ni-based adsorbents [10].
With the advances in materials science, a large number of new functional materials [17] have been emerged in the last decade, and metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) [18,19] are one of type of these materials. The pore size and shape of MOFs can be relatively easily tuned from the microporous to the mesoporous scale by altering the connectivity of the inorganic moieties and the nature of the organic linkers. MOFs generally exhibit high porosity, specific functionality, selective interaction and many other desirable properties. MOFs are currently applied in a large number of fields including adsorption and separation [20–22]. MOFs have been successfully applied also for ADS [23–25] and ADN [26,27]. So far, several types of chemical interactions have been reported to explain the adsorption mechanism [28–30] and a large number of these are based on modified MOFs.
MOFs are promising materials for adsorption-related applications not only because of their high porosity, but also because of the possibility of modifying the surfaces with specific functionalities, which leads to a specific interaction with the guest molecules and improved adsorption and selectivity [31]. Although MOFs inherently exhibit various promising physical and chemical properties, their properties can be further improved by various modifications.
Even though pristine MOFs [26] or modified MOFs with Brønsted acids have been reported for ADN [32,33], there has been no report of ADN with MOFs loaded with Lewis acid salts. One strategy involves impregnation or loading Lewis acids inside the cage of MOFs to improve the capacity of the MOFs for adsorption. Although Lewis acids such as \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\) have been utilized in acid catalyses for a long time [34], to the best of our knowledge, there has been no report in which Lewis acids have been loaded into the pore of a MOF to date.
Herein, we report the impregnation of a Lewis acid, \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\), into a MOF, MIL-100(Fe), for the first time. The impregnated MOF is applied to the adsorptive removal of basic materials from fossil fuels. QUI is utilized as a source of basic NCC materials in a model fuel containing \(n\)-octane and \(p\)-xylene as the solvent. These basic materials are one of the main contaminants in coal-derived fuels [35,36] that are expected to gain importance in the future. To clarify the acid–base effect, a neutral NCC, indole (IND), was also used for the adsorption study. An SCC, benzothiophene (BT), was also added to the fuel to investigate the selective removal of NCCs over SCCs. It was found that the MOF, modified with suitable functional groups, can be used for improved and specific applications, including ADN through acid–base interaction.
