## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Chemicals and synthesis of adsorbents
\(p\)-Xylene and \(n\)-octane were purchased from Junsei Chemical Company. Nitric Acid \(\left(\mathrm{HNO}_{3}\right.\), \(35\%\) ) and hydrofluoric acid (HF, \(48.0\%\) ) were obtained from OCI Company Ltd. Benzothiophene (BT), quinoline (OUI), indole (IND) and trimesic acid \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BTC}\right)\) were obtained from Sigma–Aldrich Co. Metallic iron powder \(\left(\mathrm{Fe}^{0}\right)\) was purchased from DC chemicals. All the chemicals in this study were used without further purification. The synthesis of the MOF, MIL-100(Fe), was carried out solvothermally under conventional electric heating [37]. The detailed procedure for the synthesis of the MOF is as follows: \(\mathrm{Fe}^{0}\) \((280\;\mathrm{mg})\), HF \((420\;\mathrm{mg})\), \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) \((705\;\mathrm{mg})\), \(\mathrm{HNO}_{3}\) \((189\;\mathrm{mg})\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \((24.9\;\mathrm{g})\) were mixed in a molar ratio of 1:2:0.67:0.6:277. The mixture was then transferred to a \(100\;\mathrm{ml}\) Teflon-lined autoclave and heated in an electric oven for \(8\;\mathrm{h}\) at \(160\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). After synthesis, the MOF was filtered, washed, and purified by stirring initially with water, and then with ethanol, and finally dried in a drying oven at \(100\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
An exact amount \(_{(0.3\,\mathrm{g}}\) each) of MIL-100(Fe) was transferred to glass vials containing different amounts of \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\). Subsequently, \(7.5\;\mathrm{ml}\) of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) was added to each vial and the mixture stirred magnetically for approximately \(1\;\mathrm{h}\) at room temperature. The mixture was then dried in a rotary evaporator to obtain a homogeneous loading of \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\) into the pores of MIL-100(Fe). The materials were denoted \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{2}(\mathsf{x}^{2})/\mathsf{M I L-100}(\mathsf{F e})\), where \(\mathsf{X}\) corresponds to the weight% of \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\) loaded into the composite.
## 2.2. Characterization
X-ray powder diffraction patterns (XRD) were obtained using a diffractometer D2 Phaser (Bruker, with \(\mathsf{C u K}\alpha\) radiation). The nitrogen adsorption analyses of the adsorbents were carried out at \(-196\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) using a surface area and porosity analyzer (Micromeritics, Tristar II 3020) after evacuation at \(150\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\;\mathrm{h}\).
## 2.3. General procedures for the adsorption experiments
Stock solutions (each at a concentration of \(10,000\;\mathrm{ppm}\)) of the model fuel were prepared separately for the three adsorbates (BT, QUI, and IND) by dissolving them in a mixture of \(75\;\mathrm{vol\%}\) \(n\)-octane and \(25\;\mathrm{vol\%}\) \(p\)-xylene. Combined solutions of varying concentrations were prepared by successive dilution and mixing of the three solutions. A solution containing a fixed concentration of NCCs and SCCs \((400\;\mathrm{ppm}\) QUI, \(400\;\mathrm{ppm}\) IND, and \(800\;\mathrm{ppm}\) BT) was used to determine the adsorption capacities at various adsorption times. To calculate the adsorption capacities of the individual adsorbates with time, solutions of QUI and IND were prepared separately at a concentration of \(1000\;\mathrm{ppm}\) each in the same solvent mixture. To obtain the maximum adsorption capacities of the adsorbents for QUI, several solutions with different concentrations \((300\;\mathrm{ppm}-1800\;\mathrm{ppm})\) of QUI were prepared using the same solvent composition. Prior to adsorption, the adsorbents were dried in a vacuum oven at \(150\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\;\mathrm{h}\) and stored in a desiccator. For each adsorption experiment, an exact amount of the adsorbent \((\sim5.0\;\mathrm{mg})\) was added to the model fuel \((\sim5.0\;\mathrm{ml})\) and stirred magnetically for a predetermined time \((15-240\;\mathrm{min})\) while maintaining the adsorption temperature at \(25\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). After adsorption, the solution was separated from the solid using a syringe filter
(PTFE, hydrophobic, \(0.5\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\)) and analyzed with a GC (DS Science, IGC 7200) equipped with a flame ionization detector.
All the adsorption capacities \(\left(\mathrm{mg/g}\right)\) were calculated from the difference between final concentration and initial concentration of an adsorbate by using the following equation:
\[
q_{t}=\frac{(C_{i}-C_{f})V}{M}
\]
where,
\(q_{t}=\) adsorbed amount in time \(t\;(\mathrm{mg/g})\) \(C_{i}=\) initial concentration of the adsorbate \(\left(\mathrm{mg/ml}\right)\) \(C_{f}=\) final concentration after adsorption \(\left(\mathrm{mg/ml}\right)\) \(V=\) volume of the solution subjected to a single adsorption (ml) \(M=\) mass of the adsorbent taken during a single adsorption (g)
The maximum adsorption capacity \(\left(Q_{0}\right)\) was calculated using the Langmuir adsorption isotherm. The adsorption isotherms for different adsorbents were plotted according to the Langmuir equation [38]:
\[
\frac{C_e}{q_e}=\frac{C_e}{Q_0}+\frac{1}{Q_0b}
\]
where,
\(C_{e}\) : the equilibrium concentration of the adsorbate \((\mathrm{mg/L})\) \(q_{e}\) : the amount adsorbed at equilibrium \(\left(\mathrm{mg/g}\right)\)
\(Q_{0}\) : the Langmuir constant (maximum adsorption capacity, \(\mathrm{mg/g}\)) \(b\) : the Langmuir constant \(\left(\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{mg}\right)\)
Therefore, the maximum adsorption capacity, \(Q_{0}\), could be obtained from the reciprocal of the slope of a plot of \(C_{e}/q_{e}\) against \(C_{e}\).
The separation factor \(\left(R_{\mathrm{L}}\right)\) was calculated using the following equation that describes the adsorption process [32]:
\[
R_{\mathrm{L}}=\frac{1}{1+bC_{o}}
\]
where,
\(R_{\mathrm{L}}\) : separation factor \(b\) : the Langmuir constant \(\left(\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{mg}\right)\) \(C_{o}\) : initial concentration of adsorbate \(\left(\mathrm{mg/L}\right)\)
## 3. Results and discussion
3.1. Textural properties and characteristics of \(\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}/\mathrm{MIL}-100(\mathrm{Fe})\)
Fig. 1(a) shows the XRD patterns of MIL-100(Fe) and other composite adsorbents along with that of \(\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}\). No \(\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}\) peaks were detected in the XRD patterns of the loaded materials, which indi
cate that \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\) was deposited in the MOFs in a uniform non-crystalline layer. Furthermore, the amount of \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\) in the composite was quite low, and thus could not be detected. The \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms (Fig. 1(b)) and pore size distribution curves (Fig. 1(c)) indicate that the loaded materials have slightly lower porosity than the pristine MOF. As shown in Table 1, a gradual reduction of the BET surface area and total pore volume was observed with increasing \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\) loading. The pore size distribution curves show a comparatively narrower distribution of pores for the loaded materials. There are two types of cages in MIL-100 materials as shown in Fig. 1(c). It was found that the loaded \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\) reduced the diameters of both type of pores without severely blocking any of them. This might be also an indication of a uniform layer formation of \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\).
## 3.2. Adsorption results
Fig. 2(a) and (b) show the adsorbed amounts of QUI and IND, respectively, with time for MIL-100(Fe) containing different amounts of \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\). The adsorption results obtained with pure \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\) are also included for comparison. The maximum QUI adsorption was achieved with \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}(2.5\%)/\mathsf{M I L-100}(\mathsf{F e})\) (Fig. 2(a)), and almost no removal of QUI was obtained with pure \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\), probably because of the low porosity. For further understanding, the adsorption results were plotted against the \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\) loading for different adsorption times (Fig. 3(a)), demonstrating that the best QUI adsorption performance was achieved with \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}(2.5\%)/\mathsf{M I L-100}(\mathsf{F e})\). At \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\) loadings higher than \(2.5\%\), the adsorption was reduced sharply, thus, the amount of QUI adsorbed was the lowest with \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}(5.0\%)/\mathsf{M I L-100}(\mathsf{F e})\). Adsorption was similarly performed for IND with various adsorbents. As shown in Figs. 2(b) and 3(b), the amount of adsorbed IND decreased with increasing \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\) loading, which is quite different from the results achieved with QUI. Similar to the case of QUI, IND was negligibly adsorbed with pure \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\). The difference in the adsorption behavior of QUI and IND can be explained
in terms of the Lewis acid–base interaction between the adsorbents and the adsorbates. Because basic NCCs such as QUI are usually hard Lewis bases [26], they tend to interact with the Lewis acidic \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\), and hence, adsorption was improved in the loaded adsorbents. On the other hand, the virgin MOF, MIL-100(Fe) itself is a Lewis acid, thus, it shows a comparatively higher adsorption capacity for QUI compared with the other adsorbates [26,32].
However, there was no particular acid–base interaction in the case of IND because this does not have any basicity. Instead, IND adsorption operates prominently by van der Waals interaction (which is generally favored by high porosity) or through H-bonding [4], or \(\pi\)-complexation [6,38]. When the MOF is loaded with \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\), the adsorption sites are partially blocked (i.e., the porosity is reduced) and hence the adsorption of IND is reduced.
Because the adsorption of QUI was highest over \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}(2.5\%)/\mathsf{M I L-100}(\mathsf{F e})\), further studies were done with this MOF to determine the other adsorption related properties, e.g., Langmuir parameters, including the maximum adsorption capacity of the pristine MOF and \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}(2.5\%)/\mathsf{M I L-100}(\mathsf{F e})\). The adsorption isotherms in Fig. 4(a) show that the adsorption improved with \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}(2.5\%)/\mathsf{M I L-100}(\mathsf{F e})\). After this, the adsorption amount was reduced for \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}(5.0\%)/\mathsf{M I L-100}(\mathsf{F e})\) mainly due to the tradeoff between increased acidity and reduced porosity as well as pore openings. The maximum adsorption capacities \(\left(Q_{0}\right)\) were calculated by using Langmuir plots (Fig. 4(b)) obtained from the adsorption isotherms. As shown in Table 1, the adsorption capacity of \(\mathsf{M I L-100}(\mathsf{F e})\) for QUI is \(357\;\mathrm{mg/g}\), whereas the adsorption capacity is \(417\;\mathrm{mg/g}\) in the case of \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}(2.5\%)/\mathsf{M I L-100}(\mathsf{F e})\). A \(17\%\) improvement in the adsorption of QUI was achieved with the use of \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}(2.5\%)/\mathsf{M I L-100}(\mathsf{F e})\), which is attributable to the Lewis acid–base interaction between \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\) and QUI. The adsorption may be further quantified by a dimensionless constant called the separation factor or equilibrium parameter \(\left(R_{\mathrm{L}}\right)\) [32]. Fig. 4(c) shows a plot for the calculated \(R_{\mathrm{L}}\) factors versus the initial concentrations of QUI.
All of the \(R_{\mathrm{L}}\) values ranged from 0 to 1, indicating a favorable adsorption for both the adsorbents [32]. Moreover, the \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}(2.5\%)/\) MIL-100(Fe) adsorbent had a lower \(R_{\mathrm{L}}\) value, indicative of more favorable adsorption [31] for this sample.
Because commercial fuels contain SCCs along with NCCs, the adsorption experiments were carried out for a combined solution where BT was used as an SCC. The concentration of BT was taken as twice (800 ppm) that of the QUI and IND (400 ppm each). The
amount of adsorbed QUI and IND from a combined solution is shown in Fig. 5. The adsorption of QUI and IND was several times higher than that of BT. BT was negligibly adsorbed (data not shown) from the combined solution in spite of its higher initial concentration compared with the NCCs, and no effect of \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\) on the BT adsorption was detected. Therefore, considering all of the experimental results, it can be deduced that NCCs are preferentially adsorbed relative to the SCCs, and basic NCCs are preferentially adsorbed on \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}/\mathsf{M I L}\!-\!\mathsf{A l O}(F e)\) due to the acid–base interaction between the acidic \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}/\mathsf{M I L}\!-\!\mathsf{A l O}(F e)\) and basic NCCs.
## 4. Conclusion
The Lewis acidic \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\) was loaded into a MOF for the first time, to show that favorable acid–base interaction is useful for the adsorptive removal of basic NCCs from model fossil fuels. The loaded materials were successfully utilized for the adsorption of a basic NCC, which showed a \(17\%\) improved uptake compared with the virgin MOF. In contrast, the amount of neutral IND adsorbed on the MOF decreased with the \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\) content of the latter because there was no favorable interaction between the neutral IND and acidic \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\) and the decreased porosity. From this study, it can be concluded that MOFs that are suitably modified with Lewis acidic materials such as \(\mathsf{A l C l}_{3}\) can be utilized to efficiently remove basic nitrogen materials from fossil fuels. This adsorptive removal of basic NCCs will be useful in the near future, especially when the coal-derived fuels will be a prominent energy source containing a large amount of basic NCCs.
